#: src/signal-handling.md:1
msgid "Signal Handling"
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:3
msgid ""
"[Signals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signal_(IPC)) are messages sent to "
"running programs to trigger specific behavior. For example, `SIGINT` is sent "
"to all processes in the terminal foreground process group when `ctrl-c` is "
"pressed."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:7
msgid ""
"`just` tries to exit when requested by a signal, but it also tries to avoid "
"leaving behind running child processes, two goals which are somewhat in "
"conflict."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:11
msgid ""
"If `just` exits leaving behind child processes, the user will have no "
"recourse but to `ps aux | grep` for the children and manually `kill` them, a "
"tedious endeavor."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:15
msgid "Fatal Signals"
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:17
msgid ""
"`SIGHUP`, `SIGINT`, and `SIGQUIT` are generated when the user closes the "
"terminal, types `ctrl-c`, or types `ctrl-\\`, respectively, and are sent to "
"all processes in the foreground process group."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:21
msgid ""
"`SIGTERM` is the default signal sent by the `kill` command, and is delivered "
"only to its intended victim."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:24
msgid ""
"When a child process is not running, `just` will exit immediately on receipt "
"of any of the above signals."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:27
msgid ""
"When a child process _is_ running, `just` will wait until it terminates, to "
"avoid leaving it behind."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:30
msgid ""
"Additionally, on receipt of `SIGTERM`, `just` will forward `SIGTERM` to any "
"running children<sup>1.41.0</sup>, since unlike other fatal signals, "
"`SIGTERM`, was likely sent to `just` alone."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:34
msgid ""
"Regardless of whether a child process terminates successfully after `just` "
"receives a fatal signal, `just` halts execution."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:37
msgid "Continuing Execution"
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:39
msgid ""
"The `[continue]`<sup>1.54.0</sup> attribute can be used to make `just` "
"continue execution even if it receives a fatal signal as long as the child "
"process it’s running exits successfully."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:43
msgid ""
"With no arguments, `[continue]` handles `SIGINT` (`ctrl-c`) so `SIGQUIT` "
"(`ctrl-\\`) can still be used to stop execution."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:46
msgid ""
"With arguments, one or more signals to handle may be given explicitly, as "
"`\"SIGHUP\"`, `\"SIGINT\"`, and `\"SIGQUIT\"`."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:49
msgid ""
"In this example, if `main.py` catches `SIGINT` and exits successfully, "
"`cleanup` will still run and `just` will exit successfully:"
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:61
msgid "`SIGINFO`"
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:63
msgid ""
"`SIGINFO` is sent to all processes in the foreground process group when the "
"user types `ctrl-t` on [BSD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Berkeley_Software_Distribution)\\-derived operating systems, including "
"macOS, but not Linux."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:68
msgid ""
"`just` responds by printing a list of all child process IDs and "
"commands<sup>1.41.0</sup>."
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:71
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/signal-handling.md:73
msgid ""
"On Windows, `just` behaves as if it had received `SIGINT` when the user "
"types `ctrl-c`. Other signals are unsupported."
msgstr ""
